  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an undirected graph with NNN nodes (numbered 111 through NNN) and MMM edges. Each node has a value; let's denote the value of node iii by AiA_iAi​. Note that the graph is not necessarily simple.\n\nA doubly increasing path is a directed path such that both the values of nodes and differences of values of nodes on this path are strictly increasing. Formally, it is a sequence of nodes p1,p2,p3,…,pkp_1, p_2, p_3, \\ldots, p_kp1​,p2​,p3​,…,pk​ for some integer (the length of this path) k≥1k \\ge 1k≥1 such that 0&lt;Ap2−Ap1&lt;Ap3−Ap2&lt;…&lt;Apk−Apk−10 \\lt A_{p_2} - A_{p_1} \\lt A_{p_3} - A_{p_2} \\lt \\ldots \\lt A_{p_k} - A_{p_{k-1}}0&lt;Ap2​​−Ap1​​&lt;Ap3​​−Ap2​​&lt;…&lt;Apk​​−Apk−1​​ and for each valid iii, nodes pip_ipi​ and pi−1p_{i-1}pi−1​ are connected by an edge.\n\nYou need to find the length of the longest doubly increasing path in the graph.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤101 \\le T \\le 101≤T≤10",
      "1≤N≤1051 \\le N \\le 10^51≤N≤105",
      "1≤M≤1051 \\le M \\le 10^51≤M≤105",
      "1≤u,v≤N1 \\le u, v \\le N1≤u,v≤N",
      "∣Ai∣≤1015|A_i| \\le 10^{15}∣Ai​∣≤1015 for each valid iii"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "The first line of the input contains a single integer TTT denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and MMM.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​.\nEach of the following MMM lines contains two space-separated integers uuu and vvv denoting that nodes uuu and vvv are connected by an edge.",
    "outputFormat": "For each test case, print a single line containing one integer — the maximum length of a doubly increasing path.",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n5 4\n1 2 3 4 5\n2 4\n1 5\n1 3\n3 5\n5 4\n1 2 3 4 5\n3 5\n1 2\n4 5\n5 2",
        "output": "2\n3",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nExample case 1: Any path with length 222 from a smaller node to a larger one is doubly increasing. There is no doubly increasing path with length greater than 222.\nExample case 2: The path 1→2→51 \\rightarrow 2 \\rightarrow 51→2→5 is doubly increasing."
      }
    ]
